<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>Science | dw.com</title><subtitle type="html">Science</subtitle><id>https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss_en_science</id><updated>2026-11-28T08:06:29Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss_en_science" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.dw.com/s-12526?maca=en-rss_en_science-22531-xml-mrss" /><link rel="via" href="https://rss.dw.com/xml/rss_en_science" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>AI goes from math dunce to genius</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d2e7a1b17ffae52001b59294fd2da5ea5c6c218dce8bfc02dc53ebb667ddb6cf</id><updated>2026-11-28T08:06:29Z</updated><published>2026-11-27T11:13:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/ai-goes-from-math-dunce-to-genius/a-80067691--cb0cc2b981f3.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/ai-goes-from-math-dunce-to-genius/a-80067691?maca=en-rss_en_science-22531-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">It once struggled to get simple sums right. But AI is now tackling tough mathematical problems like the Riemann hypothesis. How AI made this extraordinary leap — and why mathematicians should be "deeply worried."</summary><category term="Science" /></entry><entry><title>What happens if a nuclear weapon explodes in space?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ed15b1a244eeaff7fd5ce025f4b7eb688e79a1d334eb4713628ee2c013e21226</id><updated>2026-11-28T08:06:29Z</updated><published>2026-11-24T08:34:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/what-happens-if-a-nuclear-weapon-explodes-in-space/a-80025827--a6201a0063e7.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/what-happens-if-a-nuclear-weapon-explodes-in-space/a-80025827?maca=en-rss_en_science-22531-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Earth's atmosphere offers protection from a nuclear blast in space, but satellites are more exposed. We explain how radiation could damage them, why the effects can linger and what protection is possible.</summary><category term="Science" /></entry><entry><title>Rule-breaking is contagious — and good examples barely help</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:20276860cc4cb141f6dc6968b07bab408b1e5dc6e84800aeb58eb0c3d356c946</id><updated>2026-11-28T08:06:29Z</updated><published>2026-11-23T06:33:00Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.dw.com/en/rule-breaking-is-contagious-and-good-examples-barely-help/a-79980025--098488c374ac.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.dw.com/en/rule-breaking-is-contagious-and-good-examples-barely-help/a-79980025?maca=en-rss_en_science-22531-xml-mrss" /><summary type="html">Seeing someone break a rule makes people more likely to break it too, a new study has found, especially when the rule-breaker is on their side politically. Watching others behave well barely made a difference.</summary><category term="Science" /></entry></feed>
